# Engineering Rule Book

## Process
- Make no changes unless the user explicitly asks. Discuss approach first when unsure.
- Test first: list every edge case, write the failing tests, then write code to make them pass.
- Small, incremental commits with short, plain messages (e.g. "Add employee search"), no prefixes.
- Nothing is committed that fails the pipeline: RuboCop and RSpec on commit, Brakeman and
  bundler-audit on push, the full CI on GitHub. Never bypass hooks with `--no-verify`.
- Record significant decisions in the trade-offs table in `docs/design.md`.
- Keep `README.md` current when setup, commands or conventions change.

## Code style
- Keep it simple. Prefer the plain, obvious solution over a clever one.
- Files stay small and readable: aim for 100 lines, hard limit ~150. Split when it grows.
- No duplicate code. Extract shared logic into a service, concern or helper.
- Every class/module starts with a short comment saying what it is for and why it exists,
  so whoever modifies it later knows the intent. Comment non-obvious logic only.

```ruby
# Calculates the monthly earnings/deductions breakdown for one employee salary.
# All salary math lives here so rules change in one place.
class SalaryCalculator
```

## Data
- Never hard delete business data. Use soft delete (`deleted_at` timestamp).
  Hard delete only for data that will never be used again (e.g. expired tokens).
- Money is always `decimal`, never float.

## Errors
- Never swallow errors. No `rescue` in services, models or controller actions;
  let errors raise so they reach the ONE place that handles them.
- That place is the `ErrorHandling` concern on `ApplicationController`:
  - Expected errors map to 4xx responses and are logged as warnings.
  - Unexpected errors (bugs) are logged at error level with the full backtrace, reported via
    `Rails.error.report` (error tracking plugs in there), and answered with a generic 500.
    Internal details never leak to the client.
- Domain errors inherit from `ApplicationError` (`app/errors/`) and declare their status.
- Error JSON is always `{ error: "message", code: "snake_case", details: {...} }`.

```ruby
# BAD: hides the failure
def call
  salary.save!
rescue StandardError
  nil
end

# GOOD: raise a domain error; the base class renders it
raise Salaries::OverlappingPeriodError, "Overlaps an existing salary" if overlap?
```

## Security
- No raw SQL. Use ActiveRecord query methods and associations.
  Raw SQL only when truly unavoidable, always parameterised, with a comment explaining why.
